      "body_html": "looking again at the pics in this thread and elsewhere, I agree <span itemscope=\"\" itemtype=\"http://schema.org/Person\"><a class=\"user-link js-user-link user-11 group-15\" href=\"/user/11\" itemprop=\"url\" style=\"color: #136616;\" title=\"@meizzwang\"><span itemprop=\"name\">meizzwang</span></a></span> .  It may be environmental or as simple as the angle of the shots I'm taking.  This trap seems to have presented heavily on the alata side of the cross, with its bulbous shape.  I'm going to take some better shots by moving the plant when I get home but here is one I attempted to get its really dark backside:<br/><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://tubnxg.by.files.1drv.com/y4mwSPpEI0ogfbsqj8rVeblXSvtmMn7bgpOUNMkIgzKdMpYVuKYOyxV-pb2K1FdUqU09sylPlFqWiIsF2E6YRxsUPeUcuIgjzLi3U-nKd4BLP-pRGNPgUCKWA800be0di8ERKn_mF2o_yHBi5-0iGSE_NoEVl_N7zVB4sDalW8Gvw3jGZtGF21WV2CKEDEo6hkgllwEfrN_zX3B31aCZ7FI2A?width=768&amp;height=1024&amp;cropmode=none\" style=\"max-width:100%;\"/><br/><br/><br/> Also, I saw another trap forming so we'll see where that goes.",
